<section xml:id="section-polynomial-rings">
  <title>Polynomial Rings</title>
  <p>
    In <xref ref="example-poly-ring"/>, I mentioned that polynomials
    can be made ourt of any commutative ring. Let me expand on that
    with a restated complete definition of a polynomial ring. (As
    mentioned in the last section, for this and the rest of our tour
    of ring theory, I will be assuming commutativity throughout). 
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>R</m> be a ring and let <m>x</m> be a symbol unrelated
        to that ring. Then the set of all polynomials with
        coefficients in <m>R</m> and using <m>x</m> as the variable is
        a ring with notation <m>R[x]</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Similarly, if <m>y</m> is another symbol unrelated to that
        ring, I can also make the two-variable polynomial ring
        <m>R[x,y]</m>. 
      </p>
      <p>
        This can be iterated as often as you want. If the variables
        are <m>x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_k</m> then <m>R[x_1, x_2, \dots
        x_k]</m> is the <m>k</m>-variable polynomial ring. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    I've defined multi-variable polynomials and they are a pretty
    important class of rings in mathematics. However, in this course,
    I'll stick to single-variable polynomial rings, since all of the
    constructions that I want to demonstrate can happen in those rings. 
  </p>
  <p>
    The standard operations and definitions of polynomials apply
    regardless of the ring <m>R</m>. In particular, let me remind you
    of the most commonly used terms.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            The highest power of the variable is the
            <term>degree</term> of the polynomial. 
          </li>
          <li>
            If <m>p(a) = 0</m> for some ring element <m>a \in R</m>,
            the <m>a</m> is called a <term>root</term> of the
            polynomial. 
          </li>
          <li>
            A polymomial of degree <m>d</m> has at most <m>d</m> roots. 
          </li>
          <li>
            If <m>p(x) = q(x)r(x)</m>, then <m>q</m> and <m>r</m> are
            <term>factors</term> of <m>p</m> and <m>p</m> is a
            <term>multiple</term> of both <m>q</m> and <m>r</m>.
          </li>
          <li>
            An element <m>a \in R</m> is a root of a polynomial if
            and only if <m>(x-a)</m> is a factor of the polynomial. If
            <m>(x-a)^k</m> is a factor of the polynomial and <m>k</m>
            is the largest exponent for which this is true, then
            <m>k</m> is the <term>multiplicity</term> of the root. 
          </li>
          <li>
            Each expression <m>p_ix^i</m> is a <term>term</term> of the
            polynomial. The base ring element <m>p_i</m> is a
            coefficient. 
          </li>
          <li>
            If <m>p_dx^d</m> is the term with the higest exponent in the
            polynomial, then <m>r</m> is called the <term>leading
            coefficcient</term>. The leading coefficient is always
            non-zero. If the leading coefficient is <m>1</m>, then the
            polynomial is a <term>monic</term> polynomial. 
          </li>
          <li>
            A <term>constant polynomial</term> is an element <m>a</m>
            of the base ring thought of as a polynomial with no other
            terms.  Equivalently, it is a polynomial of degree 0. As a
            particular case, <m>0</m> is the zero polynomial. 
          </li>
          <li>
            If <m>a \in R</m>, then taking a polynomial <m>p(x)</m>
            and replacing <m>x</m> with <m>a</m> produces <m>p(a)</m>,
            an element of the base ring. This is called the
            <term>evaluation</term> of the polynomial at <m>a</m>. 
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    Let me state some of the first properties of general polynomial
    rings. I'm going to restrict a little again here and only talk
    about polynomials over domains; again, all of the deveopment that I
    want to do can take place in this situation and the definitions
    can be stated more clearly when I don't have to worry about zero
    divisors. 
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>R[x]</m> be a polynomial ring over a base ring <m>R</m>
        which is a domain. 
        <ul>
          <li>
            The units of <m>R[x]</m> are the units of the base
            ring: <m>R^*</m>. 
          </li>
          <li>
            <m>R[x]</m> is also a domain.          
          </li>
          <li>
            There is always an injective ring homomorphism <m>i: R
            \rightarrow R[x]</m> which sends a ring element to itself
            considered as a constant polynomial. 
          </li>
          <li>
            For every <m>a \in R</m>, there is a surjective evaluation
            homomorphism <m>\pi_a: R[x] \rightarrow R</m> which sends
            all polynomials to their evaluation at <m>a</m>. 
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    These definitions and results means that working with
    polynomials over a domain feels pretty familiar. Most of what
    you are accustomed to for real polynomials still works, and I'll
    try to carefully point out what is different. 
  </p>
  <p>
    In ring theory, I am concerned about factoring. This leads me to
    one of the most important definitions for polynomial rings. 
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>R[x]</m> be a polynomial ring over a domain. A
        polynomial <m>p(x)</m> is called <term>irreducible</term> if, 
        in any product of factors <m>p(x) = q(x)r(x)</m>, at least one
        of the factors is a constant polynoial. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    You may have used this term already to talk about real
    polynomials. Let me review that in an example. 
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      In <m>\RR[x]</m>, there are two types of irreducible
      polynomials. 
      <ul>
        <li>
          All polynomiials <m>(ax + b)</m> at irreducible. (This is
          true of any polynomial ring over a domain.) 
        </li>
        <li>
          All polynomials <m>(ax^2 + bx + c)</m> such that <m>b^2 -
          4ac \lt 0</m> are irreducible. these are the degree two
          polynomial without real roots. 
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <p>
      In <m>\RR[x]</m>, there are no irreducible polynomials of degree
      three or higher. Any such polynomials will have either a linear
      of a quadratic irreducible factor. This is not true in general. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    Let me do some examples which show how this can vary in other
    polynomial rings. 
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      The polynomial <m>x^2 - 2</m> is irreducible in <m>\QQ[x]</m>.
      If this were a real polynomial, it would factor as
      <m>(x-\sqrt{2})(x + \sqrt{2})</m>. Such a factorization is
      impossible in <m>\QQ[x]</m>, since <m>\sqrt{2}</m> not a
      rational number. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Similar, the polynomial <m>x^4 - 2</m>, as a real polynomial,
      would factor as follows. 
      <me>
        x^4 - 2 = (x - \sqrt[4]{2})(x + \sqrt[4]{2}) (x^2 + \sqrt{2})
      </me>
      This factorization cannot be done in <m>\QQ</m>, so this is,
      again, an irreducible polynomial over <m>\QQ</m>. This shows
      <m>\QQ[x]</m>, unlike <m>\RR[x]</m> can have irreducibe
      polynomials of degree higher than two. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    There is, then, a substantial difference in irreduble
    polynomials between <m>\RR[x]</m> and <m>\QQ[x]</m>. What about
    <m>\ZZ[x]</m>? Here, there is surprisingly little difference. To
    demonstrate, first a need a definition.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>p(x) \in \ZZ[x]</m> be a polynomial. I'll write its
        expansion with terms and coefficients. 
        <me>
          p(x) = p_0 + p_1 x + p_2 x^2 + \ldots + p_d x^d
        </me>
        If the greatest common divisor of all the <m>p_i</m> is 1,
        then the polynomial is called <term>primitive</term>. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    If a polynomial isn't primitive, then a factor can be pulled out
    of all the coefficients and the remaining polynomials will be
    primitive. For example, <m>3x^2 + 12 x + 15</m> is not primitive,
    but can be written as <m>3(x^2 + 4x + 5)</m> and <m>x^2 + 4x +
    5</m> is primitive. For the sake of factoring and irreducibility,
    then, we often just care about primitive polynomials, even over
    <m>\ZZ</m> where I cannot technically remove the three by dividing
    by it. 
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <title>Gauss' Lemma</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>p(x) \in \ZZ[x]</m> be a primitive polynomial. Then
        <m>p(x)</m> is irreducible in <m>\ZZ[x]</m> if and only if it
        is irreducible in <m>\QQ[x]</m>. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        One direction here is easy: if a polynomial is irreducible in
        the larger ring <m>\QQ[x]</m>, it cannot factor in the smaller
        ring either, so it is irreducible in <m>\ZZ[x]</m>. 
      </p>
      <p>
        For the other direction, assume that the polynomial is
        irreducible in <m>\ZZ[x]</m>. For the sake of contradiciton,
        assume it is not irreducible in <m>\QQ[x]</m>. That means
        there are rational non-constant polynomails <m>q(x)</m> and
        <m>r(x)</m> such that this factorization is true. 
        <me>
          p(x) = q(x) r(x)
        </me>
        Now let <m>a</m> be the lcm of the denominators of the
        coefficients of <m>q(x)</m>, and likewise <m>b</m> for
        <m>r(x)</m>. This means <m>aq(x)</m> and <m>br(x)</m> are
        integral polynomials, since I have cleared all denominators.
        Then I can rewrite the equation.
        <me>
          ab p(x) = (aq(x)) (br(x))
        </me>
        It looks like I am almost done this proof, since all the
        polynomials here are integral. There is a subtlety here, which
        I'm going to skip in this proof, about common factors of the
        coefficients of integral polynomials. (The proof usual
        involved reductions modulo primes, which is a neat and fairly
        common technique for statements about the integers.) The
        subtlety allows me to say that, since <m>p(x)</m> is
        primitive, the product <m>ab</m> must actually be <m>\pm
        1</m>, and I can divide by it. 
        <me>
          p(x) = \pm 1(aq(x)) (br(x))
        </me>
        The result is a decomposition in <m>\ZZ[x]</m>. This
        contradicts the irreducibility of <m>p(x)</m> in the integers.
        By contradiction, I conclude that irreducibility over the
        integers implies irreducibility over the rationals. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    For degree two and three polynomials, the polynomial is
    irreducible if and only if it has a linear factor, which means it
    has a root. Outside of this observation, though, it's pretty
    difficult to tell when a polynomial is irreducible. Therefore, we
    have some criteria to help. I'll state these criteria here for
    integral polynomails, but I'll extend them to other rings in a
    future section.
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>p(x) \in \ZZ[x]</m>. 
        <me>
          p(x) = p_0 + p_1 x + p_2 x^2 + \ldots + p_d x^d
        </me>
        I've written <m>p(x)</m> in its expansion with <m>p_d \neq
        0</m>. I'll also assume <m>p_0 \neq 0</m> (since otherwise
        <m>x</m> is a factor and the polynomial is immediately
        reducible).  Then there following are useful criteria about
        roots and irreducibility.
        <ul>
          <li>
            If <m>a \in \ZZ</m> is a root of <m>p(x)</m>, then
            <m>a</m> is a factor of <m>p_0</m>. As noted above, a
            polynomial of degree three or less is irreducible if and
            only it has no roots. 
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
            If there exists a prime number <m>j</m>  with these two
            conditions:
            <ul>
              <li>
                <m>j</m> is a factor of all of the <m>p_i</m> except
                <m>p_d</m>; and
              </li>
              <li>
                <m>j^2</m> is not a factor of <m>p_0</m>;
              </li>
            </ul>
            then the polynomial is irreducible. This is called
            Eisensten's Criterion. 
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </proposition>
  <p> 
    This gives some tools to check the irreducibility of polynomials
    over the integer. Here are some examples, consider entirely in
    <m>\ZZ[x]</m> (or in <m>\QQ[x]</m>, since all examples are
    primitive). 
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <m>x^d - p</m> for <m>p</m> a prime number satisfies
          Eisensten's criterion, so this is always irreducible for
          <m>d \geq 1</m>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <m>x^3 + 6x^2 - 7x + 4</m>. This is a degree three
          polynomial, so it irreducible only it is does not have a
          root. The only possible roots are <m>\pm 1, \pm 2, \pm
          4</m>, since the root has to define the constant
          coefficient. By exhaustive check, none of these are roots,
          so this is irreducible. 
        </li>
        <li>
          <m>x^7 + 4x^5 + 12x^3 + 16x^2 + 2</m> is irreducible by
          Eisenstein's criterion using the prime number 2. 
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    Why do we care so much about irreducible polynomials? Well,
    irreducible polynomials are something that cannot be factored in
    to smaller pieces, in a way. This is something like the prime
    numbers, and indeed, the irreducible polynomials will end up
    playing a very similar role. The next section of these notes will
    develop the idea further. 
  </p>
</section>
